208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (For more than fifteen years, the prevailing thesis on Indian outbound travel has been that Indians are a price-led market. Tour operators, NTOs, and global travel platforms have built their India strategies around this assumption: discount packages, low-cost carrier partnerships, and trade-led B2B engagement.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 145 0 R
/T (The data in this report tells a different story.)
/E (The data in this report tells a different story.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (When 4,200 Indian outbound-eligible travellers were asked, in their own language, across 12 cities spanning T1, T2, and T3, what actually triggers a decision to consider a new destination, the dominant answer was not price. It was not convenience. It was not even reviews. It was inspiration: the emotional, narrative, often visual moment when a destination moves from invisible to imagined.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (67% of respondents identified inspiration \(defined as a piece of creator content, a film or OTT scene, a friend's travel story, or a piece of editorial\) as the primary reason they began considering a new destination over the last 18 months. Price came second. Convenience, third. Destination marketing campaigns ranked sixth.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (This finding has significant implications for how National Tourism Organisations, global travel brands, and Indian travel platforms should design their India strategy over the next five years. It also explains, with clarity that did not previously exist, why some destinations have captured the Indian imagination while others, with equal or greater objective merit, have not.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
